//! **The Agent Client Protocol (ACP), v1** — for apps that may carry exactly one
//! dependency.
//!
//! ACP connects an EDITOR to an AGENT. That makes it perpendicular to MCP, which
//! connects an agent to TOOLS, and the difference is not just topological: in ACP
//! the client owns the filesystem and the terminal, and the agent must ASK
//! (`fs/read_text_file`, `session/request_permission`). Permission is a message,
//! not a policy an agent applies to itself.
//!
//! # Why this is not the official SDK
//!
//! `agent-client-protocol` is excellent and unusable here. It depends on `futures`,
//! `async-io`, `async-process`, `rustix`, `schemars` and `uuid`; a deployable
//! Ferrite app may depend on `ferrite-app` and nothing else, and the code gate
//! matches on dependency NAME, so even a workspace path dependency is refused. The
//! SDK remains the reference this is written against and the harness it is verified
//! with — just from outside the app.
//!
//! # Zero preconditions
//!
//! Nothing here reads a clock, draws entropy, or performs I/O. Every value that
//! cannot be computed — the time, a session id, the bytes of a reply — arrives as a
//! parameter. So this package drops into any constrained app, and every rule in it
//! is testable without a network.
//!
//! # Layout
//!
//! | Module | What it holds |
//! | --- | --- |
//! | [`proto`] | the JSON-RPC 2.0 envelope both directions share, and the error vocabulary |
//! | [`transport`] | the HTTP/SSE and WebSocket binding: connection ids, session routing |
//! | [`schema`] | ACP v1 types: initialize, capabilities, sessions, content, updates |
//! | [`agent`] | the AGENT role — what an editor may ask of us |
//! | [`client`] | the CLIENT role — what we may ask of an agent, and what it asks back |
//! | [`session`] | session lifecycle and the state a connection holds |

